# Q : What are essential things to consider when creating a new Angular project for a company? The Angular project will be using Figma that was built on top of "Material Design 3" as its foundation. The project will be created in Azure DevOps environment. The project needs to be developed reasonably quickly and maintained long-term. I am the only developer on the project and will be working on the project all by myself.

For a **solo developer building a company Angular application that needs to ship quickly but remain maintainable for years**, I would deliberately avoid building an elaborate architecture. The goal should be a project that is **easy for you to understand six months from now, easy for another developer to inherit later, and hard to accidentally make messy**.

Angular's current guidance strongly favors standalone components, feature-oriented organization, and keeping components focused on presentation. ([Angular][1])

## 1. Start with the right architectural philosophy

I would use this principle:

> **Keep the application simple at the beginning, but make the boundaries clear enough that it can grow.**

For a solo developer, I would **not** start with:

- Micro-frontends
- Nx monorepo unless there is a concrete need
- A custom design-system framework
- Multiple state-management libraries
- Excessive abstraction layers
- A giant `shared/` directory
- Dozens of Angular libraries
- Enterprise-level ceremony around every component

Those things can be useful in large organizations, but they can slow down a one-person project substantially.

A good starting stack would be roughly:

**Angular**
→ Standalone components
→ Signals + RxJS
→ Angular Router
→ Angular Material where appropriate
→ Your Figma design system layered on top
→ REST/API services
→ Unit/component testing
→ ESLint + formatting
→ Azure DevOps Git + Pipelines
→ Automated CI/CD

Angular's current documentation describes standalone components as the default and provides migrations specifically around standalone APIs, which makes this a sensible foundation for a new application. ([Angular][2])

---

# 2. Establish your Figma → Angular strategy before writing much UI

This is probably the **most important architectural decision** in your particular project.

You have:

**Figma**
→ based on **Material Design 3**
→ Angular application

Don't treat Figma as something you manually reproduce screen-by-screen.

Instead, establish a hierarchy:

```text
Figma Design System
        ↓
Design Tokens
        ↓
Angular Theme / CSS Variables
        ↓
Angular Material
        ↓
Company UI Components
        ↓
Feature Components
        ↓
Pages
```

This prevents every developer—or future you—from inventing slightly different versions of the same button, spacing, typography, dialog, etc.

### Design tokens

Identify the Figma tokens for things such as:

- Primary / secondary / tertiary colors
- Surface colors
- Text colors
- Error / warning / success
- Typography
- Font sizes
- Font weights
- Line heights
- Border radius
- Spacing
- Elevation
- Breakpoints

Then establish an Angular representation of those tokens.

For example:

```css
:root {
  --app-color-primary: ...;
  --app-color-surface: ...;
  --app-color-text: ...;

  --app-spacing-xs: ...;
  --app-spacing-sm: ...;
  --app-spacing-md: ...;

  --app-radius-sm: ...;
  --app-radius-md: ...;
}
```

The exact implementation depends on how the Figma system is delivered.

The important rule is:

> **Don't scatter literal design values throughout components.**

Avoid:

```css
margin: 13px;
color: #3478db;
border-radius: 7px;
```

when those values are supposed to represent company design decisions.

---

# 3. Decide what belongs to Angular Material vs your company design system

This distinction is extremely important.

You shouldn't automatically build your own replacement for every Material component.

A sensible hierarchy is:

### Use Angular Material directly

For things like:

- Button
- Checkbox
- Radio
- Select
- Input
- Dialog
- Menu
- Tooltip
- Progress indicator
- Tabs
- Snackbar

when the Material component already satisfies the design.

### Wrap/customize Material components

When your company's design requires a different API or appearance.

For example:

```html
<company-button variant="primary"> Submit </company-button>
```

internally implemented with Material.

### Create truly custom components

Only when the business/design requirement isn't adequately served by Material.

For example:

```text
account-summary
customer-status
transaction-timeline
portfolio-card
```

This gives you a very important separation:

```text
Material
   ↓
Company UI
   ↓
Business UI
```

rather than:

```text
Every screen individually styles Material components
```

That second approach tends to become difficult to maintain.

---

# 4. Create a small internal design-system layer

I would probably create something like:

```text
src/
  app/
    core/
    shared/
      ui/
        button/
        card/
        ...
      directives/
      pipes/
    features/
      dashboard/
      customers/
      reports/
```

But I would **not** put every reusable thing into `shared`.

A good rule is:

> Something belongs in shared code only when it is genuinely reusable across multiple features.

For example:

```text
shared/ui/
  company-button
  company-dialog
  page-header
  loading-indicator
  empty-state
```

Whereas:

```text
features/customers/
  customer-table
  customer-filter
  customer-details
```

should remain with the customer feature.

Angular's style guide explicitly recommends organizing code around **feature areas**, rather than creating broad directories based solely on technical type such as `components`, `services`, and `directives`. ([Angular][1])

---

# 5. Organize by feature, not by technical layer

I strongly recommend something like:

```text
src/
  app/
    core/
      auth/
      http/
      configuration/
      logging/

    shared/
      ui/
      pipes/
      directives/

    features/
      dashboard/
        dashboard.routes.ts
        pages/
        components/
        services/

      customers/
        customers.routes.ts
        pages/
        components/
        services/

      reports/
        reports.routes.ts
        pages/
        components/
        services/

    app.routes.ts
    app.config.ts
```

rather than:

```text
components/
services/
models/
pages/
interfaces/
utils/
```

The latter looks organized initially but becomes painful as the application grows.

Feature-oriented organization means that when you're working on Customers, most of what you need is in:

```text
features/customers/
```

Angular itself recommends grouping closely related files and organizing application code by feature areas. ([Angular][1])

---

# 6. Keep `core` extremely small

For a solo project, `core` should mean:

> Things that represent application infrastructure rather than business features.

For example:

```text
core/
  auth/
  http/
  configuration/
  logging/
  guards/
```

Potentially:

```text
core/
  api/
  error-handling/
```

But don't dump generic reusable code here.

A classic Angular mistake is ending up with:

```text
core/
  helpers/
  utilities/
  managers/
  common/
  base/
  shared/
```

which eventually becomes a junk drawer.

---

# 7. Be intentional about state management

This is an area where I would **avoid overengineering heavily**.

You already have Angular Signals and RxJS.

For most applications, start with:

### Local UI state

Use signals:

```ts
readonly isLoading = signal(false);
readonly selectedCustomer = signal<Customer | null>(null);
```

### Derived state

Use `computed()`:

```ts
readonly canSubmit = computed(() =>
  this.selectedCustomer() !== null
);
```

### Asynchronous streams

Use RxJS:

```ts
customers$;
```

for things like:

- HTTP streams
- autocomplete
- debouncing
- complex event streams
- cancellation
- combining asynchronous sources

### Shared application state

Only introduce a dedicated state library if the application actually demonstrates a need for one.

For a solo project I'd strongly prefer:

```text
Signals
+
RxJS
+
well-designed services
```

before automatically introducing NgRx or another state-management framework.

---

# 8. Establish an API architecture early

If this Angular application talks to a backend, don't let API calls spread throughout components.

Avoid:

```ts
@Component(...)
export class CustomerComponent {
  http = inject(HttpClient);

  loadCustomers() {
    this.http.get('/api/customers');
  }
}
```

Prefer:

```text
Component
   ↓
Feature service / facade
   ↓
API service
   ↓
HttpClient
```

For example:

```text
features/customers/
  services/
    customer.service.ts
    customer-api.service.ts
```

You can keep this lightweight.

A simple pattern is often enough:

```ts
@Injectable()
export class CustomerService {
  private readonly api = inject(CustomerApiService);

  getCustomers() {
    return this.api.getCustomers();
  }
}
```

Don't automatically create five abstraction layers around an HTTP request.

---

# 9. Put HTTP concerns in one place

Establish these early:

### Authentication

```text
auth interceptor
```

### Authorization

```text
route guards
```

### Common headers

```text
HTTP interceptor
```

### Error handling

```text
HTTP interceptor
+
central error handling
```

### Correlation/request ID

Particularly useful for enterprise applications.

Something like:

```text
Angular request
      ↓
Request ID
      ↓
Backend
      ↓
Logs
```

will make production troubleshooting dramatically easier.

---

# 10. Establish environment/configuration conventions early

Avoid scattering values such as:

```ts
https://api.company.com
```

throughout the codebase.

You want a centralized configuration strategy.

For example:

```text
configuration
  API URL
  environment
  feature flags
```

The exact implementation depends on how your company deploys Angular, but the principle is important:

> **Configuration should be externalized from business code.**

This becomes particularly valuable when you have:

```text
Development
QA
UAT
Production
```

---

# 11. Routing deserves some thought

Set up routing with lazy-loaded feature areas.

Conceptually:

```text
/
├── login
├── dashboard
├── customers
├── reports
└── settings
```

And:

```ts
export const routes: Routes = [
  {
    path: "customers",
    loadChildren: () =>
      import("./features/customers/customers.routes").then(
        (m) => m.CUSTOMER_ROUTES,
      ),
  },
];
```

This provides a clean boundary between features.

Also establish:

- Authentication guards
- Authorization rules
- 404 handling
- Route-level loading
- Breadcrumb strategy
- Document titles
- Unsaved-changes behavior where needed

---

# 12. Decide your form strategy

For most enterprise applications:

> **Reactive Forms**

are still a very practical default.

Standardize:

```text
Form creation
Validation
Error messages
Form submission
Loading state
Server-side validation
Accessibility
```

More important than the specific form API is making sure forms consistently behave.

For example, establish a company convention for:

```text
Required field
Invalid email
Server validation error
Disabled state
Submitting state
Success state
```

That belongs partly in your UI design system.

---

# 13. Accessibility should be part of the architecture

Don't treat accessibility as a final QA task.

Your Figma system should establish things such as:

- Contrast
- Focus states
- Keyboard interaction
- Touch target size
- Error states
- Screen-reader labels

Your Angular components should preserve those decisions.

Angular Material can help significantly here, but don't assume using Material automatically makes the entire application accessible.

For example:

```html
<mat-icon aria-hidden="true">search</mat-icon>
```

versus an icon that conveys actual information.

Accessibility is much easier when incorporated into your reusable components rather than fixed page-by-page later.

---

# 14. Establish testing expectations early

As a solo developer, you need tests primarily as **protection against your future self**.

I'd use three layers.

### Unit tests

Test:

- Services
- Utility functions
- Complex business logic
- Data transformations

### Component tests

Test important interactions:

```text
input
→ user action
→ component state
→ output
```

### End-to-end tests

Don't try to E2E-test everything.

Protect the most important workflows:

```text
Login
→ Search
→ Create
→ Edit
→ Submit
→ Confirmation
```

The goal isn't maximum coverage.

It's:

> **High confidence around business-critical behavior.**

---

# 15. ESLint and formatting are important when you're alone

This may sound more relevant to teams, but it is especially useful when you're the only developer.

Set up:

- ESLint
- Prettier
- TypeScript strictness
- consistent import ordering if desired
- unused-code detection

Then automate them.

For example:

```bash
npm run lint
npm run test
npm run build
```

The objective is to make the correct behavior the **default behavior**.

---

# 16. Lock down dependency management

One of the easiest long-term maintenance problems is dependency drift.

Commit:

```text
package.json
package-lock.json
```

and have CI use:

```bash
npm ci
```

rather than casually installing dependencies.

Also establish a policy for Angular upgrades.

For example:

```text
Patch → regular
Minor → planned
Major Angular upgrade → deliberate maintenance task
```

Don't let the application sit untouched for years and then attempt a giant upgrade.

---

# 17. Azure DevOps should automate the boring things

Your Azure DevOps setup should ideally give you:

```text
Git repository
       ↓
Pull Request
       ↓
Pipeline
       ↓
Install dependencies
       ↓
Lint
       ↓
Unit tests
       ↓
Build
       ↓
Artifact
       ↓
Deploy
```

Azure Pipelines explicitly supports building and testing JavaScript/Angular applications, including Angular CLI commands such as `ng test` and `ng build`. ([Microsoft Learn][3])

Even though you're the only developer, **use pull requests for your own changes** when practical.

It creates a permanent engineering record:

```text
Feature
→ PR
→ automated validation
→ merge
```

rather than:

```text
commit → production
```

---

# 18. Keep your Azure DevOps branching strategy extremely simple

For one developer I would probably use:

```text
main
  ↑
feature/*
```

For example:

```text
main
feature/customer-search
feature/dashboard
bugfix/login-error
```

I would **not** introduce GitFlow unless your organization requires it.

You don't need:

```text
develop
release
hotfix
qa
staging
production
```

branches just because they're common in large enterprises.

Let the **deployment environments** provide separation rather than multiplying Git branches.

---

# 19. Use Azure DevOps branch policies even though you're alone

This is one of the rare cases where enterprise-style controls are worth keeping.

For `main`:

```text
No direct pushes
       ↓
Pull Request
       ↓
Pipeline must pass
       ↓
Merge
```

Require at least:

- Successful build
- Successful tests
- Successful lint
- Linked work item if your organization requires it

You may technically be approving your own PR, depending on company policy, but the automation still catches problems.

---

# 20. Document the things you will forget

Because you're alone, documentation isn't primarily for your coworkers.

It's for **future you**.

I'd create:

```text
README.md
ARCHITECTURE.md
CONTRIBUTING.md
docs/
  design-system.md
  deployment.md
  testing.md
```

But keep them short.

Especially document:

### Why

Not just:

> "We use Signals."

Instead:

> "Signals are used for local synchronous UI state. RxJS is used for asynchronous streams and cancellation."

That distinction prevents architectural drift.

---

# 21. Create an ADR folder

This is one of the highest-value things you can do with very little effort.

For example:

```text
docs/adr/

0001-use-standalone-components.md
0002-use-angular-material.md
0003-state-management-strategy.md
0004-feature-based-architecture.md
0005-api-error-handling.md
```

Each document can be tiny:

```text
Decision:
Use Signals for local UI state.

Why:
We want to avoid adding a state-management framework
until application complexity warrants it.

Consequences:
Some cross-feature state will be implemented through
services/signals.
```

Six months later, this can save you a tremendous amount of time.

---

# 22. Don't create abstractions until there is evidence you need them

This is particularly important for a solo developer.

Suppose you have:

```text
CustomerService
```

Don't immediately create:

```text
ICustomerRepository
CustomerRepository
CustomerRepositoryFactory
CustomerFacade
CustomerStore
CustomerManager
```

unless your architecture actually requires them.

Start:

```text
Component
   ↓
CustomerService
   ↓
HttpClient
```

and add complexity when reality demands it.

This is one of the biggest differences between **maintainable architecture** and **over-engineered architecture**.

---

# 23. Build observability into the application

For a business application, I'd strongly consider application monitoring from the beginning.

At minimum:

```text
Errors
Performance
Failed API requests
Navigation
Critical workflows
```

Depending on your company's Azure setup, this might mean Application Insights or another approved monitoring platform.

Also establish:

```text
console.log ❌
structured logging ✅
```

for anything that needs production investigation.

---

# 24. Security should be considered from day one

At minimum establish:

- Authentication mechanism
- Authorization model
- Token handling
- API authorization
- XSS protection
- CSRF strategy where applicable
- Content Security Policy where appropriate
- Secure environment configuration
- Dependency vulnerability scanning
- No secrets committed to Git

Most importantly:

> **Never put secrets in an Angular application's source or environment files.**

Anything shipped to the browser must be assumed visible to the user.

---

# 25. Think about performance before the application becomes large

You don't need to obsess over optimization on day one.

But establish good defaults:

- Lazy-loaded routes
- `OnPush` where appropriate
- Signals where appropriate
- Avoid unnecessary subscriptions
- Avoid huge component trees
- Paginate large datasets
- Virtualize large lists when appropriate
- Optimize images/assets
- Don't download data you don't need

Angular's newer reactivity model and compiler/runtime optimizations are specifically intended to support fast applications as they grow. ([Angular][4])

---

# 26. One thing I would explicitly avoid: a giant custom UI library

Since your Figma design is based on Material 3, there is a temptation to build:

```text
CompanyButton
CompanyInput
CompanySelect
CompanyDialog
CompanyTable
CompanyCard
CompanyForm
Company...
```

for absolutely everything.

Don't.

Instead:

```text
Angular Material
       ↓
small company-specific wrappers
       ↓
business components
```

Only build a custom abstraction when it provides meaningful value.

That will save you **a huge amount of development time** as a solo developer.

