# 29. The five decisions I'd spend the most time on

For your specific situation, these are the ones that matter most:

| Decision                                          | Importance |
| ------------------------------------------------- | ---------- |
| **Figma → Material 3 → Angular theming strategy** | ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐ |
| **Feature-based application architecture**        | ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐ |
| **Azure CI/CD and deployment strategy**           | ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐ |
| **API/state/error-handling conventions**          | ⭐⭐⭐⭐   |
| **Testing and coding standards**                  | ⭐⭐⭐⭐   |

Everything else can evolve.

The **design-system integration** deserves particular attention because once dozens of screens are built on top of an inconsistent styling approach, correcting it becomes expensive.

---

## The architecture I'd personally choose for you

Given that you're working alone and want **speed + long-term maintainability**, my default would be:

```text
                   ┌─────────────────────┐
                   │       Figma         │
                   │    Material 3       │
                   └──────────┬──────────┘
                              ↓
                   ┌─────────────────────┐
                   │ Design Tokens / CSS │
                   │ Angular Material    │
                   └──────────┬──────────┘
                              ↓
                   ┌─────────────────────┐
                   │ Company UI Layer    │
                   │ Small + reusable    │
                   └──────────┬──────────┘
                              ↓
        ┌─────────────────────┴─────────────────────┐
        ↓                     ↓                     ↓
   Dashboard              Customers              Reports
     Feature                Feature               Feature
        │                     │                     │
        └─────────────────────┼─────────────────────┘
                              ↓
                     Angular Services
                              ↓
                         HttpClient
                              ↓
                            API
```

with:

```text
Angular
Standalone components
Signals + RxJS
Reactive Forms
Lazy-loaded features
Angular Material
Strict TypeScript
ESLint
Unit/component tests
Targeted E2E tests
Azure DevOps Git
Azure Pipelines
Automated deployment
Monitoring
Short ADRs
```

That is a very good **"one developer can move quickly without creating a future disaster"** architecture.

One final point: **don't confuse enterprise architecture with a lot of architecture**. Since you're the only developer, the best architecture is usually the smallest architecture that gives you strong boundaries, repeatable conventions, automated quality checks, and an easy path to add features. Angular's own guidance emphasizes consistency, feature-oriented organization, focused components, and avoiding unnecessarily complicated code, which lines up particularly well with that approach. ([Angular][1])

